      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I've noticed this in a few images as well. Looks like really bad ISO noise leading to having to denoise images in lightroom or photoshop. If this noise was added intentionally, it should really be reconsidered. Noise should NEVER be added automatically like this to images as removing it is destructive by nature, but adding noise in post is not. As a photographer for most of my adult life, seeing this in images from a virtual camera is aggravating, it doesn't make the image more realistic, it just makes it look worse. Most of my photography has been done using a Nikon D800E, a fairly mid range prosumer camera, and in order to get noise this strong, I'd have to be shooting with some stupid high ISO settings which you'd simply never do outside of shooting high speed sporting events, cars racing, etc., in low lighting. When shooting portraits, scenery, still life, parties/events, you try and keep the ISO as low as physically possible in order to shoot at a usable frame rate at your desired aperture for how much movement is happening but these images look like someone set their ISO to 100K so they could have a super fast shutter speed to offset the wide aperture which is simply dumb, especially on a virtual camera. Typically you'd start with the lowest ISO your camera can do (ISO 50, ISO 100, etc) and then use the slowest shutter speed possible for your aperture/scene only bumping up ISO when you simply can't slow the shutter down anymore. \nTLDR: Get rid of the stinking noise. You're making everyone look like bad photographers using cellphones.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
